{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R4c94P8PMnAnDuoZeEAdyN","type":"tool_result","content":"# offline replica, data as of 2026-06-01T00:00:00Z\nKAFKA-20552: Support log segments larger than 2 GB\nstatus:\tOpen\nresolution:\t(none)\npriority:\tMajor\nassignee:\t(none)\nreporter:\tHaifeng Chen\nissuetype:\t(none)\ncomponents:\tcore\nlabels:\t(none)\nfixVersions:\t(none)\ncreated:\t2026-05-05T17:24:34Z\nupdated:\t2026-05-07T05:35:22Z\npull requests:\t(none)\ncomments:\t3\n\n--- description\nThe {{log.segment.bytes}} broker config (and its topic-level synonym {{{}segment.bytes{}}}) is currently defined as {{{}ConfigDef.Type.INT{}}}, capping the maximum segment size at {{Integer.MAX_VALUE}} (2,147,483,647 bytes, ~2 GB). Additionally, the {{.index}} file format stores physical file positions as 4-byte signed integers, which also cannot address beyond ~2 GB.\n\nWith modern storage hardware (multi-TB NVMe drives) and high-throughput workloads, the 2 GB cap is increasingly a problem:\n * {*}Excessive file handle usage{*}: Each segment needs 4 files ({{{}.log{}}}, {{{}.index{}}}, {{{}.timeindex{}}}, {{{}.txnindex{}}}). A 10 TB partition with 2 GB segments means ~20,000 open files.\n * {*}Frequent segment rolls{*}: A topic ingesting 500 MB/s rolls a new segment every ~4 seconds, amplifying index build, flush, and cleaner overhead.\n * {*}More log cleaning / compaction work{*}: More segments means more compaction cycles with more small groups.\n * {*}Remote storage overhead{*}: Each segment is an individual unit for tiered storage copy/delete operations.\n\nAllowing segments of 4 GB, 8 GB, or larger would significantly reduce these overheads for high-throughput, large-retention workloads.\n\nKIP https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-1333%3A+Support+log+segments+larger+than+2+GB","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4802c9b5-800f-47ba-947e-0314ab62989a","uuid":"cbdf3eb4-788c-4750-8c31-c04d10ea7b82","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:43:16.789Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# offline replica, data as of 2026-06-01T00:00:00Z\nKAFKA-20552: Support log segments larger than 2 GB\nstatus:\tOpen\nresolution:\t(none)\npriority:\tMajor\nassignee:\t(none)\nreporter:\tHaifeng Chen\nissuetype:\t(none)\ncomponents:\tcore\nlabels:\t(none)\nfixVersions:\t(none)\ncreated:\t2026-05-05T17:24:34Z\nupdated:\t2026-05-07T05:35:22Z\npull requests:\t(none)\ncomments:\t3\n\n--- description\nThe {{log.segment.bytes}} broker config (and its topic-level synonym {{{}segment.bytes{}}}) is currently defined as {{{}ConfigDef.Type.INT{}}}, capping the maximum segment size at {{Integer.MAX_VALUE}} (2,147,483,647 bytes, ~2 GB). Additionally, the {{.index}} file format stores physical file positions as 4-byte signed integers, which also cannot address beyond ~2 GB.\n\nWith modern storage hardware (multi-TB NVMe drives) and high-throughput workloads, the 2 GB cap is increasingly a problem:\n * {*}Excessive file handle usage{*}: Each segment needs 4 files ({{{}.log{}}}, {{{}.index{}}}, {{{}.timeindex{}}}, {{{}.txnindex{}}}). A 10 TB partition with 2 GB segments means ~20,000 open files.\n * {*}Frequent segment rolls{*}: A topic ingesting 500 MB/s rolls a new segment every ~4 seconds, amplifying index build, flush, and cleaner overhead.\n * {*}More log cleaning / compaction work{*}: More segments means more compaction cycles with more small groups.\n * {*}Remote storage overhead{*}: Each segment is an individual unit for tiered storage copy/delete operations.\n\nAllowing segments of 4 GB, 8 GB, or larger would significantly reduce these overheads for high-throughput, large-retention workloads.\n\nKIP https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-1333%3A+Support+log+segments+larger+than+2+GB","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
